SECTION A — ANCHOR VERIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(A-a) hall2010.txt  ->  Michael J.W. Hall, "Local deterministic model of
singlet state correlations based on relaxing measurement independence"
[title completed 2026-08-26 per arXiv abs page], arXiv:1007.5518v4 [quant-ph]
21 Dec 2010 (PRL 105, 250404 (2010)).

VERDICT: CONFIRMED. File is the v4 arXiv text; metric and bound match the task
brief exactly (M in [0,2]; S <= min{2+3M, 4}).

Verbatim quotes (section "Quantifying measurement independence" unless noted):
  [Eq. (3)]   "M := sup_{X,X',Y,Y'} \int d\lambda |\rho^{XY}(\lambda) -
               \rho^{X'Y'}(\lambda)|,  (3)"
  [after (3)] "i.e, by the 'maximum distance' between the distributions of the
               underlying variable for any two pairs of measurement settings.
               Clearly, a distance of M = 0 corresponds to the case of full
               measurement independence ... Conversely, suppose that M attains
               its greatest possible value, M = 2, for some model."
  [Eq. (4)]   "F := 1 - M/2.  (4)  Thus, 0 <= F <= 1 ..."
  [Sec. "Bell inequality violation", Eq. (11)]
              "For any value 0 <= M <= 2, a deterministic no-signalling model
               is constructed below which has degree of measurement
               independence M , and for which E = min{2 + 3M, 4}.  (11)"
  [optimality] "This generalisation implies in particular that the right hand
               side of Eq. (11), and the corresponding models in Tables I and
               II, are optimal, in the sense that no larger violation of the
               Bell-CHSH inequality is possible for deterministic no-signalling
               models with a given value of M."
  [singlet]   "M_singlet = 2(\sqrt{2} - 1)/3 \approx 0.276  (9)"
              "F_singlet = (4 - \sqrt{2})/3 \approx 86%  (10)   [corrected 2026-08-25: originally transcribed as (4-2\sqrt{2})/3, a typo — (4-\sqrt{2})/3 = 0.8619 matches '≈86%' and the lost fraction (\sqrt{2}-1)/3]"
  [abstract]  "... giving up a fraction of just 14% of measurement
               independence ..."

Note for RESULTS.md: Hall's M is the pairwise L1 distance between two
setting-pair source distributions (in [0,2]); F_Hall = 1 - M/2 is the fraction
of MI retained. Our metric F = max_ab TV(p(.|ab) || 1/N) measures per-setting
distance from a FIXED uniform baseline — a different object (see §C).

(A-b) branciard2020.txt  ->  arXiv:2007.11903v1 [quant-ph] 23 Jul 2020,
"Measurement-dependence cost for Bell nonlocality: causal vs retrocausal
models".

VERDICT: CONFIRMED, with one correction to the task brief: author order is
Michael J. W. Hall FIRST and Cyril Branciard second (not "Branciard & Hall").
All MI values and the ordering in the brief match the text exactly.

Verbatim quotes:
  [Eq. (31)]  "IR (SQ ) \sim 0.046 bits,  (31)  i.e., just a little more than
               1/2^2 of a bit of measurement dependence is required to reach
               the Tsirelson bound."
  [Eq. (45)]  "IC (SQ ) = I1 (SQ ) \sim 0.080 bits  (45)  is required for any
               fully causal model reproducing the maximal quantum violation."
  [Eq. (52)]  "IOS (SQ ) \sim 0.128 bits.  (52)"
  [Eq. (56)]  "ISD (S) := 2 bits  (56)  for superdeterministic models."
  [Eq. (54)]  "IZ (S) := IC (S),  (54)  for zigzag models"
  [Sec. V, Eq. (73)]
              "IR (S) < IC (S) = IZ (S) < IOS (S) < ISD (S)  (73)
               for the minimum informations required to model any given value
               S \in (2, 4) of the CHSH parameter. Recall that the subscripts
               denote retrocausal, causal, zigzag, one-sided and
               superdeterministic measurement dependence, respectively."
  [intro]     "The corresponding minimal informations are found to increase
               progressively for retrocausal, causal, one-sided, zigzag and
               superdeterministic measurement dependence ... retrocausal
               measurement dependence requires strictly less mutual information
               than any other type."
  [footnote 21] "A similar result holds for any good quantifier of measurement
               dependent correlations, including the variational distance
               quantifier M introduced in Ref. [26]."
               (Ref [26] = Hall, PRL 105, 250404 (2010).)

(A-c) bhl2026.txt  ->  G. Bacciagaluppi, R. Hermens, G. Leegwater, "Extending
Bell's Theorem: Nonlocality via Measurement Dependence", arXiv:2602.11300v2
[quant-ph] 14 Jul 2026 (dated 4 July 2026).

VERDICT: CONFIRMED. The "signalling in principle" claim is exactly as
described. It is the only paper found in the sweep using that phrase; it is
qualitative (no fine-tuning metric), so it frames but does not overlap with
our NS-silence theorem (§C).

Verbatim quotes:
  [abstract]  "We show that (analogously to violations of locality due to
               action at a distance) certain violations of this Measurement
               Independence assumption can be associated with a notion of
               signalling in principle, thus making them also testable in
               principle, and spell out the appropriate conditions.
               Accordingly, we show that by imposing no-signalling one can
               prove a version of Bell's theorem that does not require the
               assumption of Measurement Independence."
  [Sec. 5]    "We have identified conditions under which a certain form of
               non-triviality in hidden variables models leads to signalling ...
               We thus effectively show that imposing no-signalling allows one
               to derive the Bell inequalities."
  [Sec. 7 — direct context for our NS-silence result]
              "While at first striking, the fact that even though these
               approaches violate MI they do not allow for signalling in
               principle need not really be surprising, because the dependence
               of the hidden variables on Alice's and Bob's settings is
               arguably not causal ... it is possible that in all cases in
               which the hidden variables can be said to causally depend on
               Alice's and Bob's settings, Charlie has in principle the ability
               to prepare the required non-quantum distributions, thus enabling
               at least one of Alice or Bob to signal."
  [Sec. 5/6]  "Signalling in principle is thus the crucial notion that makes it
               possible to distinguish operationally between the different
               forms of nonlocality: correlations without signalling, signalling
               with a preferred time order, or signalling with no preferred
               time order."

TASK 3 — FGHK VERIFICATION (arXiv:1809.01307)
VERDICT: CONFIRMED. Full metadata (from arxiv.org/abs/1809.01307, cross-
checked against branciard2020.txt reference [9]):
  Title:    "Relaxed Bell Inequalities with Arbitrary Measurement Dependence
             for Each Observer"
  Authors:  Andrew S. Friedman, Alan H. Guth, Michael J. W. Hall, David I.
            Kaiser, Jason Gallicchio
  Versions: v1 4 Sep 2018; v2 24 Jan 2019
  Venue:    Phys. Rev. A 99, 012121 (2019)
One-line summary: tight relaxed CHSH inequalities for models in which MI is
violated to different degrees at Alice's and Bob's side (per-observer
Hall-type parameters), with saturating locally-causal constructions.
Relation to ours: class (ii) — same program (quantify MD cost of CHSH
violation), different metric family (per-observer pairwise Hall-type
distances, not TV from a fixed uniform source). No overlap with our exact
curves or NS-silence result.

SECTION B — RELATED WORKS (2015–2026 sweep)
Overlap class: i = same metric as ours; ii = different MD metric; iii =
unrelated/contextual. Threat = to OUR novelty claims.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
B-1  Hall, PRL 105, 250404 (2010); arXiv:1007.5518. [pre-window, foundational]
     Metric: pairwise L1 M in [0,2] between setting-pair sources; F_Hall =
     1 - M/2. Result: S <= min{2+3M,4}, tight; singlet needs
     M = 2(sqrt2-1)/3 ~ 0.276 (gives up 14% of MI). Class ii. Threat LOW —
     the reference metric; our F is TV from a fixed uniform baseline, not
     pairwise.

B-2  Barrett & Gisin, PRL 106, 100406 (2011); arXiv:1008.3612. Metric: mutual
     information I(X,Y:Lambda) in bits. Result: <= 1 bit suffices for the
     singlet. Class ii. Threat none/low.

B-3  Hall, PRA 84, 022102 (2011); arXiv:1102.4467. Metric: MI bits + relaxed
     inequalities; KS robustness. Result: ~1/15 bit for singlet correlations.
     Class ii. Threat low.

B-4  Thinh, Sheridan & Scarani, PRA 87, 062121 (2013); arXiv:1304.3598.
     Metric: min-entropy of source given settings. Result: trade-off bounds.
     Class ii. Threat low.

B-5  Pütz, Rosset, Barnea, Liang & Gisin, PRL 113, 190402 (2014);
     arXiv:1407.5634; and Pütz & Gisin, NJP 18, 055006 (2016);
     arXiv:1510.09087. Metric: none — full MDL polytope (MI relaxed entirely).
     Class iii/ii-lite. Threat low (no distance quantification).

B-6  Friedman, Guth, Hall, Kaiser & Gallicchio, PRA 99, 012121 (2019);
     arXiv:1809.01307. See TASK 3 above. Class ii. Threat low.

B-7  Hall & Branciard, arXiv:2007.11903 (2020). Metric: MI bits per model
     class (causal/retrocausal/zigzag/one-sided/superdet); values in §A-b.
     Class ii. Threat low — different metric; but the closest prior work on
     "minimum cost to reach 2*sqrt(2)" and must be cited in RESULTS.md.

B-8  Hossain et al., arXiv:2107.12495 (2021). Metric: Hall's M extended to
     tripartite + one-sided/bipartite variants. Result: minimum relaxation for
     Svetlichny/NS2 etc. (e.g., 41% of freedom sacrificed). Class ii.
     Threat low.

B-9  Takakura, Morisue, Watanabe & Kimura, arXiv:2208.13634 (v4 Mar 2025);
     companion: Kimura, Susuki & Morisue, arXiv:2206.06196. Metric: M(s) =
     Hall's pairwise L1 + "hidden information" H = 1 - max_lambda p(lambda).
     Result: S <= 2 + min{3M, (3/4)M + 2H, 2}, necessary & sufficient for
     factorizable models. Class ii. Threat LOW-MEDIUM — the most developed
     recent "exact trade-off" program in a Hall-type metric; still not TV
     from a fixed baseline.

B-10 Supic, Bancal & Brunner, PRA 108, 042207 (2023); arXiv:2209.02337.
     Metric: "strong" MDL — settings fixed in nearly all rounds (epsilon
     fraction). Class ii/iii. Threat low (complementary question).

B-11 Ramanathan & Liu, arXiv:2512.18513 (Dec 2025). Metric: mu-MI / epsilon-PI
     relaxations; certification robustness. Class ii/iii. Threat low
     (abstract-level review only).

B-12 NEAR-HIT — Pal, Karoliya, Sharma & Podila, arXiv:2601.08290 (13 Jan
     2026), "A Preparation Nonstationarity Loophole in Superconducting-Qubit
     Bell Tests". Metric: delta_ens = max_{(a,b),(a',b')} d_TV(pi_ab, pi_a'b')
     — pairwise TV between temporally drifting preparation ensembles (MI
     preserved; authors call it "logically distinct ... from Hall's
     measurement-dependence parameter M"). Result: |S| <= 2 + 6*delta_ens (not
     claimed tight); IBM hardware drift 3–12%. Class ii. Threat MEDIUM for the
     general idea "quantify Bell fine-tuning with total variation" — the first
     TV-based bound found in the sweep — but LOW for our specific results:
     different mechanism (drift, not setting-dependence), no fixed uniform
     baseline, no exact curves, no NS analysis. Must be cited.

B-13 NEAR-HIT — Aaron Alai, arXiv:2608.00124 (31 Jul 2026), "Exact minimum
     measurement dependence for faithful local deterministic models of
     multipartite GHZ-Mermin correlations". Metric: Hall's M (M :=
     max_{s,s'} sum_lambda |rho_s(lambda) - rho_s'(lambda)|), F := M/2.
     Result: exact F(n) = R/[2(R+1)] for n = 3..13 (F(3)=1/3, F(5)=2/5, ...);
     ceiling F <= 1/2; Table 1 lists the CHSH-at-Tsirelson consistency value
     (sqrt2-1)/3. Class ii. Threat MEDIUM — shows "exact minimum MD" is an
     active 2026 problem, but with Hall's metric and other inequalities. Must
     be cited.

B-14 NEAR-HIT (newest) — Aaron Alai, arXiv:2608.18886v2 (19/21 Aug 2026),
     "The exact price of local realism in CHSH experiments: a measurement-
     dependence-detection trade-off surface, ...". Metric: "Hall's variational
     measure" M (definition not printed in abstract; the eta_eff=1 value below
     equals half of Hall's L1 M_singlet, consistent with M = max pairwise TV
     in [0,1]). Result: closed form M(S,eta_eff) = max{0, eta_eff((S+2)eta_eff
     - 4)/6} from LP over all local strategies; at eta_eff=1 the edge
     reproduces Hall's tight bound (S <= 2 + 3*M_Hall); Tsirelson point
     certified exactly in Q(sqrt2): M(2*sqrt2, 9/10) = (27*sqrt2 - 33)/100;
     plus a "moire phase-locking" saturating mechanism. Class ii. Threat
     MEDIUM — closest prior work to "exact minimum MD for CHSH at 2*sqrt(2)",
     with Q(sqrt2) certificates like ours, but: (i) Hall-type metric, not TV
     from a fixed uniform source; (ii) model class includes detector
     inefficiency/postselection (eta_eff), which we do not; (iii) its eta=1
     floor at 2*sqrt2 is (sqrt2-1)/3 ~ 0.1381, differing from our F* =
     (sqrt2-1)/4 ~ 0.1036 because the metrics differ. Must be cited;
     RESULTS.md should explicitly disambiguate the two conventions.

B-15 Pal, Sharma & Podila, arXiv:2607.13090 (Jul 2026). Metric: "benchmark
     dependence" eta generalization; S_eta <= min{1, S_cl + eta}. Class
     ii/iii. Threat low (abstract-level).

B-16 Bacciagaluppi, Hermens & Leegwater, arXiv:2602.11300 (2026) — see §A-c.
     Qualitative "signalling in principle"; no metric. Class iii (contextual).
     Threat none for the metric; REQUIRED citation for our NS-silence framing.

