SECTION C — NOVELTY VERDICT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Question (1): Is the per-setting-TV metric F = max_ab TV(p(.|ab) || 1/N) novel?
  FINDING: No prior use found in the 2015–2026 arXiv sweep. The two closest
  objects are (i) Hall's pairwise L1 M between setting-pair sources (no fixed
  baseline; B-1, B-9, B-13, B-14), and (ii) Pal et al.'s delta_ens — the only
  total-variation-based Bell-fine-tuning bound found — which is pairwise TV
  between drifting ensembles with MI preserved (B-12). Neither measures TV
  from a fixed uniform source per setting, maximized over settings.
  VERDICT: Novel within search coverage. Hedge required: phrase as "no prior
  use found in arXiv-indexed literature through 2026-08-23", not "proven
  novel".

Question (2): Exact S_max(N,F) curves and F*(S=2*sqrt2) = (sqrt2-1)/min(N,4)?
  FINDING: No prior art in our metric. Alai 2026b (B-14) solves the closest
  problem — exact minimum MD to reach a given S in CHSH — but under a Hall-
  type convention with detector-efficiency degrees of freedom; its eta_eff=1
  floor at 2*sqrt2 is (sqrt2-1)/3 ~ 0.1381, which is NOT our F* =
  (sqrt2-1)/4 ~ 0.1036 (different metrics; Hall's own singlet value is
  M_singlet = 2(sqrt2-1)/3 ~ 0.276 in the L1 convention). The three numbers
  are easy to confuse and RESULTS.md must disambiguate all three.
  VERDICT: Novel within search coverage; cite Alai x2, Pal et al., Takakura
  et al. explicitly as the active "exact minimum MD" program in other metrics.

Question (3): NS-silence (S_NS(2,F) = 2 for all F; F*_NS(N) = F*(N) for N>=3)?
  FINDING: No prior art found. BHL 2026 (B-16) is the only "signalling in
  principle" paper and is qualitative; its Sec. 7 explicitly notes that MI-
  violating models may not signal — exactly the phenomenon our Theorems 4/5
  quantify with a metric for the first time.
  VERDICT: Novel within search coverage; cite BHL as motivation/context.

What RESULTS.md MAY claim (with the hedges above):
  - "First characterization of CHSH fine-tuning cost using per-setting TV
     distance from a fixed uniform source" — OK if phrased "to our knowledge /
     no prior use found in [stated coverage]".
  - Exact piecewise-linear S_max(N,F) for N = 2..8, global bound S <= 2+8F,
     cap F(S=4) = ceil(N/4)/N, closed form F* = (sqrt2-1)/min(N,4), and the
     NS-silence theorems — OK, with explicit citations to B-7/B-9/B-12/B-13/
     B-14 as related-but-different-metric work.

What RESULTS.md MUST hedge / add:
  - Cite Alai arXiv:2608.18886 and arXiv:2608.00124 (both Jul/Aug 2026, after
    most of the known literature) and Pal et al. arXiv:2601.08290 — all three
    are 2026 works showing exact/TV-based MD quantification is an active front.
  - State the convention difference numerically: Hall L1 M_singlet ~ 0.276;
    fraction of MI lost under Hall's F-convention = (sqrt2-1)/3 ~ 0.1381;
    Alai's eta=1 floor ~ 0.1381; our F* = (sqrt2-1)/4 ~ 0.1036. Different
    metrics, different answers — do not present ours as contradicting theirs.
  - Note that the uniform-source baseline is a modelling choice: Hall's
    pairwise metric is baseline-free, and a reviewer may ask why TV from
    uniform rather than pairwise; RESULTS.md should pre-empt this (the uniform
    source IS the MI assumption, so F measures deviation from MI directly per
    setting).

Search coverage and limits (state in any novelty claim):
  - arXiv-centric: API metadata queries + direct abs-page fetches. ~16 papers
    read at full/abstract depth; the rest catalogue-level.
  - Window 2015–2026-08-23 (foundational pre-2015 works included as context).
  - NOT covered: paywalled journal content not mirrored on arXiv; non-arXiv
    preprint servers / institutional repositories; books/theses. The WebSearch
    tool was unusable in this environment (see §D), so coverage rests on arXiv
    plus local reference lists.
  - "No prior use found" != "proven novel".

